# Load-bearing: biome.json sets `vcs.useIgnoreFile`, so biome reads THIS file to
# decide what not to lint. A rule deleted here does not only untrack a directory, it
# also hands that directory to `bun x biome check .` — including, historically, the
# 2.9 GB tree under `.pixi/envs`. `.ignore` is not used: a second ignore file is a
# second source of truth for the same decision.
#
# Note the absence of a `_*` rule. A file called `_common.sh` is a normal filename;
# the reference repository that warns about it did so because it *had* that rule.

# ── pixi ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The whole directory, not `.pixi/*` with an exception. `.pixi/config.toml` is
# generated from `pixi.toml`, which is the source of truth, so committing it would
# put a derived file in the diff of every environment change.
.pixi/

# ── sandbox transport ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Packed output from `pixi run sandbox-pack`, and the vendored tree a restore
# writes next to the code it restored. Both are large, both are reproducible from
# `.pixi-sandbox.toml`, and the second is *derived from the working tree*, so
# ignoring it is what stops a restore from showing up as a change to the repo.
.sandbox-out/
.pixi-sandbox/

# ── javascript ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo

# ── python ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# ── coverage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Written by `pixi run cov` and uploaded by CI. The XML is a build product of the
# gate, not a source file.
coverage.xml
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage/

# ── QGIS runtime state ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pytest-qgis creates one of these under each package's pytest rootdir on every run,
# which its own documentation says to expect. They are SQLite profile databases and
# a QGIS3.ini — user state, not source. Loading biome's `useIgnoreFile` means an
# unlisted `.qgis-settings/` would also be handed to the JSON linter, and the `.ini`
# files inside it are exactly the kind of machine-written config it would reformat.
.qgis-settings/

# ── editors and OS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.orig
*.rej

# Local overrides. `.env` is listed because a restored sandbox is expected to work
# without any of it, so a file this name holds is by definition a local secret.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── astro ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `docs/dist/` is already covered by the `dist/` rule above. `.astro/` is the
# generated content-collection type tree that `astro check` reads; it is a build
# product keyed on src/content, and committing it would put a derived file in the
# diff of every page added. Ignoring it also keeps it away from biome, which reads
# this file (see the header) and would otherwise format generated `.d.ts`.
.astro/
